welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Vladimir Alexandrov the be e Benson  your professor of Slavic languages and  literatures at Yale University he is an  expert on 19th and 20th century Russian  prose especially the works of Leo  Tolstoy Andre Belly and Vladimir Nabokov  and on literary theory professor  Alexandrov is the author of a number of  scholarly books and publications and has  received several notable awards today we  talk with professor Alexandrov about his  new book The Black Russian which  recently won the Macmillan Center's  Gustav Ranas International Book Prize  welcome professor Alexandrov thank you  very much let's begin with an overview  of your book tell us about it  it's a biography of an absolutely  remarkable man he was the son of former  slaves in Mississippi who became a  multi-millionaire entrepreneur in  Czarist Moscow lost everything in the  Bolshevik Revolution of 1917 and  reestablished himself in Constantinople  as the sultan of jazz  Wow and this is quite a departure for  you from the other books you have  written what led you to write it it was  an accident that changed my life and I  remember the moment very clearly because  I was preparing to teach a graduate  seminar on Russian emigre culture  between the wars and I was reading the  memoirs of a Russian singer who is very  popular in the late nineteen teens and  1920s who described how when he escaped  from the south of Russia he wound up in  Constantinople and began to perform and  I'll translate what he said in Russian  into English for our famous Moscow Negro  Philadelphia derivatives this was so  unexpected that I remember literally  closing the book and putting it down  because for various historical reasons  they were very  few black people in Imperial Russia  whether from Africa the Caribbean or the  United States  I knew the period I knew something about  the culture and I was so intrigued that  I decided to study it and this led me to  do work in archives and libraries and  six countries and was quite a trip I bet  I bet so how did he get to Russia from  Mississippi he broke the mold for people  of his background at every step of his  life when his family fell apart in  Memphis  in 1890 when his father was brutally  murdered he went north which was long  before the large migration of black  people from the south to the northern  part of the United States in 1894 he did  something else that was very much ahead  of the curve for black Americans seeking  greater freedom he went to Europe and he  picked up languages very easily  apparently enjoyed working as a waiter  in a valet which was a very portable  skill until in 1898 he came across a  rich Russian in Montecarlo who offered  him a job as a valet in st. Petersburg  and that's how Thomas his name is  Frederick Bruce Thomas came to Russia  initially and after that his personal  life and his career just skyrocketed so  you know how did he become to be so  famous I mean he sounds like a  fascinating man he was I think what he  did is first of all he was a very bright  man and very good at dealing with people  he was a wonderful psychologist and he  sort of brought the quintessence of  southern hospitality  to Russia and amplified it here an  popular entertainment venues popular  gardens that would have all kinds of  theaters and entertainments in theaters  themselves restaurants he was a great  host great attention to detail and he  was able to make a success of a business  that was a very difficult business to be  in in those days there's a lot of  competition  and was this in Russia he was doing yeah  he started out as a waiter in Russia  around 1900 by 1911 he started to take  off on his own with some Russian  partners within a year of his opening  his first business he'd made in today's  dollars over a million dollars in clear  profit which he plowed into other  investments which were also extremely  successful so for popular entertainment  light operas musical performances light  plays farces and so on a good food lots  of champagne vaudeville performances of  different kinds he was one of the  leading figures in Imperial Russia just  before it all came crashing down in 1917  was he an entertainer himself or just a  kind of a man behind it he had actually  studied singing and his family members  his sons in particular always sang later  on but he was an entrepreneur so he  wasn't the actual draw onstage but the  one who organized their own he had a  great talent for importing to Moscow  successful acts from all over Europe and  even from the United States so I'm  curious how did you manage to find out  the details of his life I was lucky that  he left a paper trail because you can  frequently come across interesting  people who for various reasons didn't  leave traces after them but Frederic  Thomas did because he was successful in  Moscow and when he got to Constantinople  which is what Istanbul used to be called  as you know he had a lot of dealings  with the American consulate general in  that city and it was my wonderful  fortune that the secretaries in that  consulate generals kept every last scrap  of paper  pertaining to Frederic Thomas's affairs  that were delivered to their offices or  that he sent to them and they're all in  the National Archives in Washington DC  so I was able to find a great deal of  information but it was fortunate that he  left the patient room  I am curious in writing a biography and  and doing the research and piecing  together someone's life do you ever  worry about getting something wrong all  the time yeah absolutely and the only  thing one can do is to be as diligent as  possible to throw your net as widely as  possible to try to cross-reference  details I one of the things that was a  great challenge but also a very  appealing part of writing this up was to  be truthful to the factors I knew them  without modifying or exaggerating  anything and so if I was unsure about  something that somebody said about him I  tried to hedge it in the way that I  expressed it without constantly saying  it seems that or one could suppose  something else  so it's cross referencing things and  being very wary as you walk the thin  edge between invention and facts so were  there people in his life at the time  that you could also research to get  information about him there were and  actually one of the most interesting  people that he was friends with was a  very famous black American boxer Jack  Johnson who had become the world  heavyweight champion in 1910 in the  United States had had a terrible time  because of the racial barriers in the  professional boxing world the white  champions wouldn't want to meet him in  the ring but when he did fight them he  won and he was constantly hounded in the  United States during the nineteen  nineteen teens and Frederick reached out  to him from Moscow when Jack Johnson was  being run out of Chicago Frederick  offered him a gig in Moscow to put on  exhibition matches which Johnson  accepted except that he didn't get to  Russia until 1914 right on the eve of  the Great War so they were able to  become friends but none of the matches  that were planned which would have been  a major event in both entertainment and  sport in Russia and in Europe never took  place  but yes Jack Johnson is very  well-documented he's a very well known  figure so I was able to find people like  that and lesser ones too  now for having been so infamous I'll say  during his life he's relatively unknown  now whereas other people like for the  boxer for instance have stood the test  of time so to speak why do you think  that is  yeah Frederick Thomas was pretty much  forgotten when he died in 1928 and my  book is the only study of him that  exists it's an attempt to bring him back  to public memory I think there are  several reasons one is American racism  we're talking about a period in American  history when the achievements of black  people were not actively celebrated by  the white press and other media of the  time also Frederick by this point was  living halfway around the world in  Turkey so he was not as omnipresent as  say Jack Johnson was in the United  States and Turkey also had changed by  this point because in 1923 the Turkish  Republic was proclaimed it turned  against being exceptionally nice to  foreigners the way the old Ottoman  Empire used to be and so even within the  new Turkish context there was no  interest in celebrating a prominent  foreigner so he fell in between the  historical cracks as it were although he  had been famous during his life one way  in which I can mention is that when  American tourists came to Constantinople  in the 1920s they all wanted to do the  same thing but tourists still want to do  their now they want to go see Hagia  Sophia they want to go to the Grand  Bazaar and in those days they all wanted  to go to Frederick Thomas's nightclub to  hear some really good jazz and to have a  really stiff drink because prohibition  was enforced in the States I say but  once he disappeared from the scene  people forgot about him so ultimately  what did happen to him his end was  tragic  he overextended himself after have  become successful in Constantinople he  opened ventures that did not succeed  there was too much competition his  creditors seized his properties he had  no protection from the Americans in  Turkey who had washed their hands of him  fundamentally for race racial reasons  and he was put in to debtors prison  where he contracted pneumonia and where  he died and he was buried in  Constantinople I know the cemetery where  he was buried and then very shortly  thereafter memory of him completely  faded well it's nice that you've brought  him back to life  no it was I really enjoyed the project  and I understand you know you have been  offered a couple of different  opportunities on the large screen  perhaps maybe in miniseries and a  musical talk about that that's that's a  lot of fun of course several individuals  and companies have expressed interest in  the possibility of either doing a  feature film or frankly what is now more  appealing is doing a TV miniseries  because that's where a lot of creative  energy is going so you know many books  are called very few are chosen so we'll  see what happens  but it's promising uh-huh what is more  certain is a musical version of Frederic  Thomas's story which was solicited from  me by an entertainment and hospitality  company in New York and over the summer  I wrote a script for that okay there are  nine songs I worked with the composer at  the company to come up with period  appropriate styles of music early 1920s  jazz Russian gypsy style music things  like that and I wrote the lyrics to the  music that he composed we went back and  forth it's going to be a kind of a  cabaret style one-man show initially  that should be staged in New York at  some point in late fall or winter and if  that seems successful the idea is to try  to develop it into a full-fledged  musical very exciting now it must have  been  I'm very interesting for you I mean I  would imagine heavy  ever written any lyrics or a script  before well I've written narrative I  hadn't really tried my hand at writing  any kind of a play like form I did write  poetry I did study poetry a lot and  analyzed it a lot in my teaching so it  turned out to be not as much of a  stretch to write the lyrics as I also  thought it would be when this first came  up and it proved to be a lot of fun I  bet the music was very well composed  that the composer was very talented in  finding just the right kind of music for  what I wanted to do and it was a very  enjoyable process well we will look  forward to that  coming out great all right thank you so  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your work thank you it's  my pleasure for more information about  Professor Alexandre and his research  please visit our website at Yale edu  slash macmillan report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

